nge because  tiny parts makes very small tolerances  we are working in micron tolerances so  you cannot do anything with a good  tuning we have in-house tool making  Department which makes from the little  screwdrivers wash maker needs up to a  stamping tool which takes months of  development so the reason why we do our  tuning in-house because if you are not  mastering your tools  you're not mastering the part you want  to produce  once all the parts are cut with  machinery they go to be cleaned just  submitted to quality control who decides  if the part is good or not to continue  to the decoration of workshop  different kind of traditional movement  decorations are plied from Geneva strive  sateen finish depending on the component  is a mechanical watch you buy today is  not the leading technology anymore it's  really crafted out you are not racing  for technological breakthrough you are  more racing for making more spectacular  work shows build a very different  relation to a mechanical version you do  to an electronic device because the day  you buy it you know that the next one  will come and that you will swap it to  getting the better one with the  mechanical watch it's really the object  as a physical object of all the handwork  which make it special and unique that  makes I think a big difference to  something more the electronic side once  these parts have been decorated their  quality checked again to see if the  decoration hasn't affect the functional  aspect of it it's always a bit of a  trick you have to decorate with not to  deteriorate the path they go to be  preassembled in a specific workshop  before going to the watchmakers set  stones for instance in two main plates  put axes on two wheels and once all  these parts have been preassembled they  arrived to watchmaker who does the final  assembly the watchmaker gets all the  little parts in little boxes start  taking the main plate which is a base on  everything gets build on adding the  wheels which are all on axes put  different bridges holding all different  wheels in place you have to add all the  winding mechanism because you want to be  able to warn you are put a dial on it  then you put hands and one last thing  which we aren't always at the end is the  escapement which is basically the heart  of any mechanical watch it's also what  you hear when you're listening to a  mechanical watch when you have the Tic  Tac it's a very first time you will see  and here you watch moving  [Music]  starting from the simple beating it's a  long process going to a highly accurate  mechanical watch you cannot just put the  parts together and expect the watch to  tell perfect time we are checking the  watches or 600 hours on different  vibration and other machines to get  really sure that everything is okay and  adjust a little tiny dust you don't see  it when you put it all together but when  you move the watch it kind of files off  the movement now this process is pretty  really long but this is what's a  complexity of such a mechanical device  requests once you see that the accuracy  of the movement is good you put it into  a watch case which we protect the  movement you add the bracelet and the  backup and you have a watch watches are  most of the time perceived as a time  capsule it's really something which are  still built today as it used to be for  the last centuries it's nice I think  also for people to be able to buy  something which has always existed and  there probably will always exist as a  form of art  [Music]  you

